# Local CI configuration (C++).
#
# Copy to .ci.env (which is gitignored) and edit what you need. This file is
# documentation: every knob below has a default in tools/ci.sh, so with no .ci.env at
# all the repo behaves exactly as described here.
#
#   cp .ci.env.example .ci.env

# Parallel build jobs for the build/asan/tsan/pristine stages.
CI_JOBS=4

# Build type for the normal, sanitizer and pristine builds.
CI_BUILD_TYPE=Debug

# Stages run when no stage names are given on the command line.
# What this repo certifies today: every stage the kit ships, tidy included (it runs against
# this repo's own .clang-tidy — the bug-finding subset; the measurement is at the end of this
# file). Both hooks run this list, and the fast tier is still "build tests".
CI_DEFAULT_STAGES="tree format kitprobes build tests version asan tsan tidy pristine"

# 1 = a missing tool (clang-format, clang-tidy) FAILS the run instead of SKIPping it.
# Set to 1 on a machine that is supposed to have them, so a silently missing gate
# cannot pass unnoticed. This repo's `format` stage needs clang-format: a machine meant
# to run the whole gate wants CI_STRICT_TOOLS=1.
CI_STRICT_TOOLS=0

# 1 = keep the pristine-checkout temp directory after a run (its path is printed).
CI_KEEP_TMP=0

# Where per-stage output goes. Gitignored; on failure the tail is printed and the path
# is named. Every one of these paths must be in .gitignore: the tree stage fails when
# the gate's own footprint would show up as an untracked file.
CI_LOG_DIR=.ci-logs
# The kit's build dirs, unchanged. Build output never appears in this repo's history:
# .gitignore covers build/ and build-*/, and `tools/ci.sh tree` fails if that stops
# being true (the OLD build/ tree was committed here until 2026-09-20 — INCIDENTS.md).
CI_BUILD_DIR=build
CI_ASAN_BUILD_DIR=build-asan
CI_TSAN_BUILD_DIR=build-tsan

# The source set the format and tidy stages own (a space-separated list of git pathspecs).
CI_SOURCE_GLOBS="'*.cpp' '*.cc' '*.cxx' '*.hpp' '*.hh' '*.h'"

# Extra -D flags for every configure in this script (dependencies, options, presets).
# CI_CMAKE_FLAGS="-DMYPROJECT_BUILD_TOOLS=ON"

# The test runner, given to `eval` with $CI_BUILD_DIR and $CI_JOBS expanded at run time.
# ctest is the portable default; a project that does not register tests with add_test()
# points this at its own suite instead:
#   CI_TEST_CMD="./\$CI_BUILD_DIR/my_tests --quiet"
CI_TEST_CMD="ctest --test-dir \$CI_BUILD_DIR --output-on-failure -j \$CI_JOBS"

# Where the SECOND copy of the version number lives — the file the version stage
# compares against project(<name> VERSION x.y.z) in CMakeLists.txt. Two shapes are
# both fine:
#
#   generated by CMake (recommended):
#     # CMakeLists.txt
#     project(myapp VERSION 0.1.0 LANGUAGES CXX)
#     configure_file(cmake/version.hpp.in ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/generated/version.hpp @ONLY)
#     # cmake/version.hpp.in
#     #pragma once
#     #define APP_VERSION "@PROJECT_VERSION@"
#
#   tracked by hand:  CI_VERSION_HEADER=include/myapp/version.hpp
CI_VERSION_HEADER="$CI_BUILD_DIR/generated/version.hpp"

# Binaries that must answer `--version` with the same number (a glob, expanded by the
# shell). Unset means the binaries are not asked — every project names them differently.
CI_VERSION_BINARIES="$CI_BUILD_DIR/permuto"

# The THIRD copy has no knob, on purpose: stage_version finds the generated CMake package
# version file itself ($CI_BUILD_DIR/*ConfigVersion.cmake, written by
# write_basic_package_version_file in CMakeLists.txt) and requires the same number. A knob
# would only create a way to leave the copy unchecked; if CMakeLists.txt calls that macro
# and no file is found, the stage fails instead of skipping. Why: INCIDENTS.md, 2026-09-20.

# clang-tidy baseline — OPTIONAL. With no file, the tidy stage requires ZERO findings.
# Create one only to tolerate findings you inherited, and create it with the gate:
#
#   tools/ci.sh --write-tidy-baseline
#   git add .ci/tidy-baseline.txt && git commit -m "ci: accept the inherited clang-tidy findings"
#
# That one command runs the build stage (tidy refuses to run without
# $CI_BUILD_DIR/compile_commands.json — the build stage is what writes it), runs tidy, and
# writes every finding it reports into this file, then prints the `git add` line: the
# baseline is part of the repo, and the tree stage fails on a file that is neither
# committed nor ignored. Its tidy stage failing during that capture is expected — with no
# baseline yet, every inherited finding counts as new.
#
# Do NOT capture the file by hand from .ci-logs/tidy.log. The comparison normalises BOTH
# sides (tidy_key in tools/ci.sh): the repo root is stripped from every path, and
# `:line:column` is stripped, so one finding is still one finding after code moves above it,
# and a baseline captured in one clone still describes a checkout at another path (the
# nightly clean checkout, a colleague's machine). A raw copy of the log matches none of
# that, so every baselined finding reads as new and the tidy stage is permanently red — it
# cannot pass at all, on any machine. There is exactly one definition of the form, and it
# lives in the script; ask the script for the file.
#
# One honest consequence of the line-blind comparison: a SECOND identical finding in a file
# that already has one collapses into the first. Fix the baselined finding rather than
# growing the baseline around it.
# Permuto has no baseline file: the check set in this repo's .clang-tidy (the bug-finding
# subset) is clean on this tree, and a baseline is for findings you inherited, not for a rule
# set nobody agreed to. Adding one here would make the tidy stage tolerate something on
# purpose — see INCIDENTS.md.
CI_TIDY_BASELINE=.ci/tidy-baseline.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PERMUTO NOTES — what is wired, and the rule set `tidy` runs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WIRED, on every push (.githooks/pre-push -> tools/ci.sh):
#   --require-clean tree format build tests version asan tsan tidy pristine
#
#   tree    — the OLD build/ tree used to be committed (130 of the 169 tracked files were
#             CMake output), which is exactly why this stage could not pass. It was
#             untracked on 2026-09-20 and .gitignore now covers build output, so the stage
#             is wired: a repo that starts committing build output again fails immediately,
#             and the "never publish an uncommitted tree" rule is back in the stage
#             (--require-clean) instead of living in the hook.
#   format  — .clang-format now exists (the suite house style: LLVM base, 4-space indent,
#             100 columns, the same file Computo/JSOM/jsonTools use) and the whole tree
#             was formatted in one mechanical commit, so the stage is wired. It checks the
#             files a branch touches (the stage's own scoping, unchanged from the kit).
#   version — the identity check now covers every copy of the number this repo has:
#             project(VERSION) in CMakeLists.txt, the generated version.hpp, the generated
#             *ConfigVersion.cmake that a downstream find_package() reads, and the
#             --version output of the binary. They are all one edit apart in
#             CMakeLists.txt; desynchronising any copy fails the stage.
#
# NOT WIRED — nothing: `tidy` is in the list above, and this is the rule set it runs:
#
#   .clang-tidy in this repo enables the BUG-FINDING subset — clang-diagnostic-* (the
#   compiler diagnostics clang-tidy sees while parsing) plus clang-analyzer-* (the Clang
#   Static Analyzer) — and no style families. Zero findings are required: there is no
#   .ci/tidy-baseline.txt here, and a baseline is for findings you inherited, not for a rule
#   set nobody agreed to.
#
# WHY THAT SET AND NOT A HEAVIER ONE — measured 2026-09-20 on this tree, 17 translation
# units, 4 cores, so the next reader does not have to re-measure it:
#
#   * the stock families (-*,bugprone-*,performance-*,readability-*,modernize-*,
#     portability-*), the set this decision was FIRST measured against: 451 findings in
#     387 s — 220 modernize-use-trailing-return-type, 136 modernize-use-nodiscard. Style,
#     which .clang-format plus review already own.
#   * the suite's value-only set (-*,bugprone-*,performance-*), which Computo, JSOM and
#     jsonTools run: 53 findings in 204 s here. 9 are the known
#     bugprone-unchecked-optional-access false positives on GoogleTest
#     ASSERT_TRUE(opt.has_value()) guards; 11 are ONE decision about the published API
#     (performance-enum-size wants a narrower base type for MissingKeyBehavior, reported once
#     per translation unit that includes the header). Adopting it here is an OPEN decision
#     with these numbers attached, not a rejected one.
#   * the analyzer set this repo wired: 0 findings — and the one finding it reported before the
#     fix was REAL (the unread round-trip flag in examples/api_example.cpp, which made the
#     example print "✗ Round-trip integrity failed!" and then report success and exit 0). It
#     needs no exclusion list and no baseline, which is why it is the one that shipped.
#
#   The cost is real and it is paid on push, not on commit: `tidy` measured 104 s (warm) to
#   201 s (cold, right after a fresh build) over the 17 translation units on 4 cores, against
#   the rest of the gate's ~37-66 s — the whole 9-stage gate ran in 141 s with warm caches. The
#   fast tier is still `build tests`, and this is the cheapest tidy in the suite (JSOM's is
#   ~620 s). Recorded here, in CODING_STANDARDS.md, in tools/ci.sh and in INCIDENTS.md — where
#   a future reader looks first.
